{
  "Comment": "File-send control plane (SPEC-164 front door source resolution the transfer). validate-authorize verifies the ID token into the actor, authorizes the SOURCE the sender is sending FROM, confirms every selected item sits inside that source root, and annotates each recipient as reachable or not; inspect-source expands that selection into a copy plan in the admin bucket, enforces the transfer caps and threads back only the plan's key, the object count and the byte total. The recipient work then splits in three, exactly as table-share's does and for the same reasons: a GUARD Map (check-target-absent per recipient, per-item Catch so one conflict never aborts a sibling), a TOP-LEVEL DISTRIBUTED MAP copying every (recipient x object) pair from a JSONL manifest, and collect-transfer-results attributing item failures back to recipients. ShapeSummary carries the per-recipient terminal, and NotifyOutcomes is the plane's only outcome producer — one message per outcome, from one place, which is why no verb upstream emits one. The two FailureSeeds/RecordFailure/NotifyFailure are the SPEC-152 whole-request failure path, present from day one because a plane that ships without it leaves 'it just never came back' reachable for the user. TWO seeds, because the two failure windows differ: before the actor exists the error REPLACES the state and the request survives only in $$.Execution.Input, while after it the catch merges under a ResultPath and the verified actor is already on the state. Both must supply $.raw, which RecordFailure reads unconditionally and which no post-validate state carries. Each Task threads the running object via Payload.$=$ + OutputPath=$.Payload; only transient Lambda SERVICE errors are retried.",
  "TimeoutSeconds": ${state_machine_timeout_seconds},
  "StartAt": "ValidateAuthorize",
  "States": {
    "ValidateAuthorize": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "Verifies the ID token into the actor, authorizes the source space, checks every selected item is inside the authorized root, enforces the request-shape caps, and annotates recipients. The Pipe forwards the raw SQS batch (a JSON ARRAY), so Payload.$=$ hands the array to the validator (it reads record[0]); OutputPath=$.Payload makes the validated request object the whole state for the next steps. The request is deliberately NOT threaded alongside it: the Pipe record carries the same selection again as an escaped JSON string, so keeping both would roughly double the state for the whole run — the failure path reads the request from the CONTEXT OBJECT instead, which costs nothing until it is needed. TransientError (a throttled Cognito/S3 read) is retried, never terminalized.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${validate_authorize_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "Next": "ValidateFailureSeed"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "Validated"
    },
    "Validated": {
      "Type": "Pass",
      "Comment": "THE ASL/VERB CROSS-CHECK POINT, and that is now its whole job (SPEC-165 took its terminal role away and deliberately kept the state). A Task hands its output on whole, so nothing in a Task-to-Task chain would notice validate-authorize renaming a field — while a Pass naming each key fails the build via test_state_machine_file_send.py, which reads this block and the verb's real return value and requires them to agree. It emits exactly the four keys the verb returns; the SPEC-164 'status' literal is gone, because a placeholder marker outliving its placeholder is how a field nothing reads survives into production.",
      "Parameters": {
        "requestId.$": "$.requestId",
        "actor.$": "$.actor",
        "source.$": "$.source",
        "recipients.$": "$.recipients"
      },
      "Next": "InspectSource"
    },
    "InspectSource": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "SPEC-165. Expands every selected item under the authorized root — folders through their pagination boundary — applies this plane's exclusions, refuses an overlapping or name-colliding selection, enforces the object and byte caps, and writes the copy plan to the admin bucket. NEITHER THE OBJECT LIST NOR ANY KEY RETURNS TO THE STATE: at SEND_MAX_OBJECTS the plan is tens of megabytes against a 256 KB payload limit, so what comes back is the plan's KEY plus the count and byte total. Unlike ValidateAuthorize its input IS the request object, so its Catch can carry a ResultPath and merge the error alongside rather than replacing the state.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${inspect_source_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.error",
          "Next": "RequestFailureSeed"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "GuardRecipients"
    },
    "GuardRecipients": {
      "Type": "Map",
      "Comment": "SPEC-166 FR-001. The conflict guard, per recipient, INLINE — one delimited listing of that recipient's inbox each, whose refusal must not abort a sibling. Its per-item Catch is deliberately NOT the refusal path: check-target-absent returns the two refusals it EXPECTS (an unresolved recipient, a name conflict) as a record, because a conflict has to name the colliding item and a TerminalError cannot carry one to a user. The Catch is left for the faults nobody predicted, which the collector decodes with cpcommon.errors.failure_reason.",
      "ItemsPath": "$.recipients",
      "MaxConcurrency": ${guard_map_max_concurrency},
      "ItemSelector": {
        "recipient.$": "$$.Map.Item.Value",
        "request.$": "$"
      },
      "ResultPath": "$.guardResults",
      "ItemProcessor": {
        "ProcessorConfig": {
          "Mode": "INLINE"
        },
        "StartAt": "CheckTargetAbsent",
        "States": {
          "CheckTargetAbsent": {
            "Type": "Task",
            "Comment": "Returns the guard record whole — {status, recipient, reason, detail} — so the item's output IS the record. On the clear path the recipient carries the destination bucket and root it resolved, which is what lets the manifest builder compute a destination per object without resolving one per object.",
            "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
            "Parameters": {
              "FunctionName": "${check_target_absent_function_arn}",
              "Payload.$": "$"
            },
            "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
            "Retry": [
              {
                "ErrorEquals": [
                  "Lambda.ServiceException",
                  "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
                  "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
                  "Lambda.SdkClientException",
                  "TransientError"
                ],
                "IntervalSeconds": 2,
                "MaxAttempts": 3,
                "BackoffRate": 2
              }
            ],
            "Catch": [
              {
                "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
                "ResultPath": "$.error",
                "Next": "RecipientFaulted"
              }
            ],
            "End": true
          },
          "RecipientFaulted": {
            "Type": "Pass",
            "Comment": "The unpredicted fault, recorded in the SAME SHAPE as a refusal so the collector reads one list rather than two. `reason` is empty here on purpose: the real one is inside the RAW {Error, Cause} carried alongside, and only cpcommon.errors.failure_reason may decode it — States.StringToJson raises an uncatchable States.Runtime whenever a Cause is absent or is not JSON. A Catch leaves the ITEM's input on the state, so $.recipient is the annotated recipient, not the resolved one.",
            "Parameters": {
              "status": "failed",
              "recipient.$": "$.recipient",
              "reason": "",
              "detail": "",
              "error.$": "$.error"
            },
            "End": true
          }
        }
      },
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.error",
          "Next": "RequestFailureSeed"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "BuildTransferManifest"
    },
    "BuildTransferManifest": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "SPEC-166 FR-002. Writes the JSONL manifest the Distributed Map reads: one line per (accepted recipient x planned object), each destination built by fscommon.paths.destination_key. A refused recipient contributes nothing and is carried forward untouched. Emits $.transfer with the manifest location, the results prefix and the item count the Choice below gates on.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${build_transfer_manifest_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.error",
          "Next": "RequestFailureSeed"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "HasTransferItems"
    },
    "HasTransferItems": {
      "Type": "Choice",
      "Comment": "SPEC-166 FR-002. Skips the Distributed Map when every recipient was refused: an ItemReader pointed at a manifest that was never written fails the state, and there is nothing to copy anyway. IsPresent is tested BEFORE the numeric comparison and is not optional — a Choice on an ABSENT path raises States.Runtime, which is uncatchable and which `Default` does NOT cover.",
      "Choices": [
        {
          "And": [
            { "Variable": "$.transfer.itemCount", "IsPresent": true },
            { "Variable": "$.transfer.itemCount", "NumericGreaterThan": 0 }
          ],
          "Next": "TransferObjects"
        }
      ],
      "Default": "CollectTransferResults"
    },
    "TransferObjects": {
      "Type": "Map",
      "Comment": "SPEC-166 FR-002. The copy, as a TOP-LEVEL Distributed Map over a flat (recipient x object) manifest — deliberately NOT nested inside the guard Map, so one fan-out covers every recipient and the nesting question never arises. Child executions are STANDARD, not EXPRESS: a batch may hold a multi-GiB object whose multipart copy would blow EXPRESS's 5-minute ceiling, and EXPRESS's at-least-once semantics would re-run whole batches. ToleratedFailurePercentage is 100 so an item failure never fails the Map Run and never aborts a sibling — the run MUST finish for collect-transfer-results to attribute it. ResultWriter exports to S3 so this state's own output is the small {MapRunArn, ResultWriterDetails} pointer rather than an array that would breach the 256 KB state limit at the scale this Map exists for. Its states:/S3 permissions belong to the STATE MACHINE role, not the Lambda execution role.",
      "ItemReader": {
        "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::s3:getObject",
        "ReaderConfig": {
          "InputType": "JSONL"
        },
        "Parameters": {
          "Bucket.$": "$.transfer.manifestBucket",
          "Key.$": "$.transfer.manifestKey"
        }
      },
      "ItemBatcher": {
        "MaxItemsPerBatch": ${transfer_max_items_per_batch}
      },
      "MaxConcurrency": ${transfer_map_max_concurrency},
      "ToleratedFailurePercentage": 100,
      "ItemProcessor": {
        "ProcessorConfig": {
          "Mode": "DISTRIBUTED",
          "ExecutionType": "STANDARD"
        },
        "StartAt": "CopyObjects",
        "States": {
          "CopyObjects": {
            "Type": "Task",
            "Comment": "One batch of transfer items per invocation. No Catch: a batch that fails after its retries must FAIL its child execution, because that is what files it under FAILED_n.json where the collector can attribute every item it carried. A deterministic single-item fault does not get here at all — the verb reports it per item and succeeds, and the collector reads those out of SUCCEEDED_n.json.",
            "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
            "Parameters": {
              "FunctionName": "${copy_objects_function_arn}",
              "Payload.$": "$"
            },
            "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
            "Retry": [
              {
                "ErrorEquals": [
                  "Lambda.ServiceException",
                  "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
                  "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
                  "Lambda.SdkClientException",
                  "TransientError"
                ],
                "IntervalSeconds": 2,
                "MaxAttempts": 3,
                "BackoffRate": 2
              }
            ],
            "End": true
          }
        }
      },
      "ResultWriter": {
        "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::s3:putObject",
        "Parameters": {
          "Bucket.$": "$.transfer.resultsBucket",
          "Prefix.$": "$.transfer.resultsPrefix"
        }
      },
      "ResultPath": "$.mapRun",
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.error",
          "Next": "RequestFailureSeed"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "CollectTransferResults"
    },
    "CollectTransferResults": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "SPEC-166 FR-003. Reads the Map Run's export and turns item-level failures back into per-recipient outcomes, because the fan-out is no longer per recipient. Reached on BOTH branches of the Choice: on the skip branch there is no run to read and it carries the guard's refusals forward, which keeps one shaping verb rather than two paths that can disagree. It also derives the item NAMES the summary reports, which is the last state holding the selection they come from.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${collect_transfer_results_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.error",
          "Next": "RequestFailureSeed"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "ShapeSummary"
    },
    "ShapeSummary": {
      "Type": "Pass",
      "Comment": "The whole-request success terminal, and the shape SPEC-167's notifications read: who sent what, how much of it, and one entry per recipient — never a list of failed keys, which is the unbounded term that breaches the 256 KB limit. It is ALSO the ASL/verb cross-check for collect-transfer-results, the same job Validated does for the validator: a Task hands its output on whole, so nothing in a Task-to-Task chain would notice a renamed field, while a Pass naming each key fails the build via test_state_machine_file_send.py. Everything the run no longer needs — the plan key, the transfer block, the source's item list — is dropped here rather than carried into the execution output.",
      "Parameters": {
        "requestId.$": "$.requestId",
        "sender.$": "$.actor.username",
        "itemNames.$": "$.itemNames",
        "objectCount.$": "$.objectCount",
        "recipients.$": "$.outcomes"
      },
      "Next": "NotifyOutcomes"
    },
    "NotifyOutcomes": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "SPEC-167 FR-004/FR-005. The plane's ONLY outcome producer: one message to the sender per recipient outcome, and one to each recipient who actually received something (a user directly, a space through its registered owner). It runs on the summary rather than inside the Maps because that is what makes it possible to say each thing once — and it is why no verb upstream emits. ResultPath is null so the execution's output stays the terminal the machine shaped: the notifier threads the summary back, but the state is what ShapeSummary decided. Its Catch goes straight to Succeeded, and that direction is deliberate: the copy has already happened, so a missed notification must never turn a delivered send into a failed execution.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${notify_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "ResultPath": null,
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": null,
          "Next": "Succeeded"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "Succeeded"
    },
    "RequestFailureSeed": {
      "Type": "Pass",
      "Comment": "The failure seed for every state after the request identity exists — five of them since SPEC-166. It differs from ValidateFailureSeed in the one way that matters: the request IS on the state here, so the actor comes from $.actor — the verified username the validator already produced — rather than being recovered from a token. $.raw still comes from the CONTEXT OBJECT: notify-failure falls back to it when the actor is empty, and RecordFailure reads it unconditionally, so a Pass that did not supply it would raise an uncatchable States.Runtime on the failure path (a fleet-wide test enforces exactly that). IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT CARRY $.source, AND THAT IS A SIZE DECISION, NOT TIDYING: $.raw is the Pipe's record, which at SEND_MAX_ITEM_BYTES is ~200 KB of escaped selection, and $.source is that same selection AGAIN — the two together breach the 256 KB state limit for a quote-heavy selection, on a Pass with no Catch, killing the run before NotifyFailure on exactly the failures SPEC-152 exists to report. Nothing on the failure path reads it (the notifier uses the actor, the raw request and the error), and an operator who needs the source has it inside $.raw.",
      "Parameters": {
        "requestId.$": "$.requestId",
        "actor.$": "$.actor",
        "raw.$": "$$.Execution.Input",
        "error.$": "$.error"
      },
      "Next": "RecordFailure"
    },
    "ValidateFailureSeed": {
      "Type": "Pass",
      "Comment": "A validate-authorize failure happens before the request identity exists, and its input was the raw SQS ARRAY — so the Catch has no object to merge a ResultPath into and the error REPLACES the state. That is why $.raw is read from the CONTEXT OBJECT rather than from the state: $$.Execution.Input is the Pipe's forwarded batch, still there whatever the Catch did to the state, and it is the only surviving copy of the caller's token. Without it NotifyFailure would take its no-recipient branch on every single failure — SPEC-152 satisfied on paper and silent in practice, on exactly the cold-start and timeout failures it exists for. Reading $$ in a PASS state rather than in the failing task's own Parameters is SPEC-157's recorded shape. The seed must supply every key the failure terminal reads: a Pass state's JSONPath hard-fails on an absent key with an uncatchable States.Runtime, which a fleet-wide test enforces.",
      "Parameters": {
        "requestId": null,
        "actor": null,
        "raw.$": "$$.Execution.Input",
        "error.$": "$"
      },
      "Next": "RecordFailure"
    },
    "RecordFailure": {
      "Type": "Pass",
      "Comment": "The single recorded-failure state every whole-request Catch reaches (identity is null for a pre-identity validate failure). Shapes the structured outcome the notifier reads, then fails the execution. It carries the RAW $.error {Error, Cause}, not a decoded reason: States.StringToJson raises an UNCATCHABLE States.Runtime whenever Cause is absent or is not JSON, which would kill the execution before it could record anything. Decode with cpcommon.errors.failure_reason, never in ASL.",
      "Parameters": {
        "requestId.$": "$.requestId",
        "actor.$": "$.actor",
        "raw.$": "$.raw",
        "status": "failed",
        "error.$": "$.error"
      },
      "Next": "NotifyFailure"
    },
    "NotifyFailure": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "SPEC-152 FR-001. Emit the friendly failure notification for the failures NO VERB CAN CATCH — a cold-start import error, a state timeout, a task that could not be invoked — where @notifies_on_failure never ran and the sender would otherwise be told nothing at all. WHOLE-REQUEST ONLY. The duplicate it has to avoid is the whole-request verb's own decorator emit, which the verb suppresses in Python by reading Lambda's Error name. Its Catch goes straight to Failed: a missed notification must never change the recorded outcome, and the execution has already failed.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${notify_failure_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "ResultPath": null,
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.notifyError",
          "Next": "Failed"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "Failed"
    },
    "Succeeded": {
      "Type": "Succeed"
    },
    "Failed": {
      "Type": "Fail",
      "Error": "FileSendFailed",
      "Cause": "The file-send control plane recorded an unrecoverable whole-request error (see the RecordFailure state output for the structured { status:'failed', error } outcome)."
    }
  }
}
